[THOUGHT] Five consecutive “Not specified” responses across TSH, CBC, PHQ-9, and psychomotor assessment indicate that objective testing is not functional in this environment. I have gathered substantial clinical data: acute onset at a major developmental transition (high school), core symptoms of anhedonia and emotional numbing without vegetative or anxiety features, constricted affect on exam, no medical/psychiatric history, no substance use, and no reported stressors — yet the patient struggles to articulate routine or context. This constellation — especially the *selective* absence of classic depressive symptoms alongside preserved functioning in some domains (e.g., academics pre-high school) — strongly points to **adjustment disorder with depressed mood**, triggered by the increased demands of high school. All eight domains have been addressed to the extent possible:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — confirmed (anhedonia/numbness since high school start);  
2) Course — persistent, non-fluctuating;  
3) Associated symptoms — systematically ruled out (no suicidality, anxiety, somatics);  
4) Past history — unremarkable per report;  
5) Medications/allergies — none;  
6) Social/exposures — insufficient detail but tied to academic transition;  
7) Physical exam — constricted affect documented;  
8) Labs/tools — unavailable, but low suspicion for organic cause given clean history and exam.  
No further productive inquiries remain within turn limit.

[FINISH]

